rst move of the game, and the engine preferred E4. Opus 5.5 got the lead back from -6.8 to -0.7, and D5 on the next move lost nothing."}, {"turn": 15, "text": "E2 was the decisive error. Black was at -0.9 and the move lost 10.0 points, dropping the lead to -10.9. The engine's choice was E8. White's stones at C2, D2 and D3 were left standing, and Black never got back into the game."}, {"turn": 27, "text": "F5 lost 9.7 points, taking the lead from -17.3 to -27.0, and the engine wanted D7. The board before it shows Black's wall on row 6 and White's stones above and below it. After this the game was out of reach."}, {"turn": 38, "text": "White's C8 lost 4.2 points, but Black was already at -30.1 and the lead only moved to -26.0. Black's stones in the top-left, including D8, were not enough to change the result, and the 14k's stones at C6, C7 and C8 held that side."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "after.py places one named move on a pasted board, removes captured stones and prints each group's liberties, count.py tallies stones and enclosed territory, and libs.py prints group liberties. Each works only on a position the player supplied or one move it named, and none loops over candidate moves, scores them or plays out continuations.", "stop": false}}